%erg v1
Title: UX P2: resolve author identity from git config instead of hardcoded claude
Created: 2026-05-05
Author: haduong

--- log ---
2026-05-05T00:00Z haduong created

--- body ---
## Context

Item B from ticket 0071 triage. `erg new` writes `Author: claude` into every
generated ticket file. `erg close` writes `claude closed —` and
`claude note blocker` into log lines. These are wrong for any non-Claude actor.

Resolution order (first non-empty wins):
1. `git config user.name` — via `os/exec`, matches repo identity
2. `$ERG_AUTHOR` env var — opt-in override
3. `$USER` env var — OS fallback
4. `"unknown"` — last resort

## Actions

1. Add a `resolveAuthor() string` helper in a shared file (e.g. `util.go` or
   inline in `new.go`). Implement the four-step fallback chain above.
2. In `new.go` template: replace `"Author: claude"` with `"Author: " + resolveAuthor()`.
3. In `new.go` log line: replace `"claude created"` with `resolveAuthor() + " created"`.
4. In `close.go`: replace `"claude closed —"` and `"claude note blocker"` with
   `resolveAuthor() + " closed —"` and `resolveAuthor() + " note blocker"`.

## Test

In `tests/test_new.sh`: `ERG_AUTHOR=testuser erg new "foo"` → ticket contains
`Author: testuser` and log line contains `testuser created`.

In `tests/test_close.sh`: `ERG_AUTHOR=testuser erg close ID done` → log line
contains `testuser closed`.

Unit test for `resolveAuthor()`: mock env vars, verify fallback chain.

## Exit criteria

- `make test` passes.
- `grep -r '"claude"' tickets/tools/go/*.go` returns no matches inside
  author-identity strings.
- `ERG_AUTHOR=alice erg new TITLE` produces `Author: alice`.
- Running without `ERG_AUTHOR` in a git repo produces the git user name.
